          "text": "**Observations:**\n- **Inputs outside a 20×20 box are untouched.** The first 112 inputs (rows 0–3) and the last 112 (rows 24–27) have weights of at most about ±0.036. The same holds for 8 of the 28 inputs in every row in between. That range matches the default random initialisation (±1/√784 ≈ 0.036), so these pixels were never non-zero in training.\n- **Location of the box.** Only a 20×20 block (rows 4–23, twenty consecutive columns, probably columns 4–23) carries trained weights. The exact column offset is inferred from the repeating 20-then-8 pattern, not from a precise count. The images are therefore 20×20 content padded with an empty border.\n- **The box edges matter.** Inside the box, the edge rows and columns carry large weights, up to about ±0.6. The objects therefore often reach the edges of the 20×20 frame, both vertically and horizontally. They look scaled to fill the box, unlike MNIST digits, which keep their aspect ratio.\n- **Hidden unit 2: a sharp centre-column line.** Its weights are strongly negative (about −0.42 to −0.57) on a single column at the image centre, from roughly row 16 to row 23. They are weaker (about −0.1 to −0.3) on that column from row 14 and on the column next to it. This is a very sharp, consistent dark line in the middle of the lower half.\n  - Its output weights are strongly positive for classes 1, 5, 7 and 9 (+0.83, +0.83, +0.73, +0.62).\n  - They are negative for classes 2, 3, 4, 6 and 8.\n- **Hidden unit 0: width and top of the object.** Its weights are strongly negative along the left edge columns in rows about 8–12. They are positive along the top row (row 4) near the centre. This unit responds to objects that are narrow but reach the top.\n  - Its output weights favour classes 1, 3 and 7 (+0.50, +0.89, +0.71).\n  - They penalise classes 0, 4, 6 and 9 (−0.61, −0.41, −0.49, −0.62).\n- **Overall shape of the features.** Weights are broad and smooth, organised as silhouettes, edges and gaps rather than thin pen strokes.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- **Hidden unit 2 fits clothing, not digits.** In MNIST a \"1\" puts ink exactly on that centre column, so the unit would suppress class 1, yet its output weight for class 1 is strongly positive. A dark gap at the centre of the lower half is instead the gap between trouser legs, which is class 1 in Fashion-MNIST.\n  - The classes this unit favours also fit. Sandals, sneakers and ankle boots (5, 7, 9) are wide, short objects with little ink in that spot.\n  - The classes it disfavours fit too. Pullovers, dresses, coats, shirts and bags (2, 3, 4, 6, 8) are filled there.\n- **Hidden unit 0 also fits clothing.** Sleeves fill the left edge for T-shirts, coats and shirts, which is why it penalises those classes. Trousers and dresses are narrow but reach the top row, which is why it favours them.\n- **Other datasets are ruled out:**\n  - MNIST and EMNIST: the class evidence above contradicts them, and digits do not fill the frame edge to edge.\n  - KMNIST: same objection, and its characters fill the full 28×28.\n  - CIFAR and SVHN: they are colour images.\n- **One caveat.** Standard Fashion-MNIST uses the full 28×28 frame. The hard empty 4-pixel border suggests this copy was resized to 20×20 and zero-padded to 28×28, in the style of MNIST.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando clothing images, 28×28 grayscale), apparently in a variant whose 20×20 content is centred in a blank 4-pixel border.\n\n**Classes:**\n- 0: T-shirt/top\n- 1: Trouser\n- 2: Pullover\n- 3: Dress\n- 4: Coat\n- 5: Sandal\n- 6: Shirt\n- 7: Sneaker\n- 8: Bag\n- 9: Ankle boot\n\n**Confidence:** 60%",
